.TH "ET\-RADIO" "1" "November 2026" ""
.SH NAME
et\-radio \- select a radio for EmComm Tools
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]et\-radio\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]et\-radio\f[R] tells EmComm Tools which radio is about to be
connected.
It does not detect or configure the radio.
Device detection and background configuration begin only when the radio
is connected.
.PP
By design, EmComm Tools supports only one radio and one application
(operating mode) at a time.
.PP
Run \f[B]et\-radio\f[R] during initial setup and whenever you want to
switch radios.
Use the up and down arrow keys to select a radio from the list of
supported equipment.
The selected radio is displayed under INTERFACES > Radio in the System
Information display.
.PP
Once a radio is selected, configuration instructions specific to that
radio will be displayed.
These instructions assume the radio has been restored to its factory
default settings.
.PP
EmComm Tools automatically manages rig control and audio settings for
supported application modes, including JS8Call and fldigi.
.SH RADIO CONFIGURATION
Before switching radios, always close the current operating mode
application, then unplug the connected radio.
.PP
To configure and connect a supported radio:
.IP "1." 3
Run \f[B]et\-radio\f[R] and select the desired radio.
.IP "2." 3
Turn on the radio.
.IP "3." 3
Configure the radio using the displayed instructions.
.IP "4." 3
Connect the radio to the computer using a USB cable.
.PP
If the radio is detected successfully, the System Information display on
the desktop should update within 10 seconds.
.PP
The selected radio is displayed under INTERFACES > Radio.
The CAT status should show Connected, and the detected audio device
should appear next to Audio.
.SH PLUG\-AND\-PLAY
EmComm Tools automatically starts the rig control service
(\f[B]rigctld\f[R]) when a supported radio\(cqs USB interface is
connected.
.PP
The service is stopped automatically when the USB cable is disconnected.
.PP
Do not manually change rig control or audio settings in supported
applications.
EmComm Tools manages these settings as part of its plug\-and\-play
subsystem.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
et\-radio
.EE
.SH WHEN TO RUN
Run \f[B]et\-radio\f[R] when:
.IP \(bu 2
Setting up a supported radio for the first time.
.IP \(bu 2
Switching to a different radio.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
If a supported radio does not work during initial setup, restore the
radio to its factory default settings.
Run \f[B]et\-radio\f[R], select the radio, and follow the displayed
configuration instructions.
.PP
If a previously working radio stops working:
.IP "1." 3
Close the current operating mode (e.g., JS8Call).
.IP "2." 3
Unplug the radio from the computer.
.IP "3." 3
Plug the radio back into the computer.
.IP "4." 3
Verify that \(lqRadio\(rq, \(lqCAT\(rq, and \(lqAudio\(rq appear in the
System Information display on the desktop.
.IP "5." 3
Start an operating mode.
